    @* ============================== Proof strip ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-proof">
        <div><strong>Written in C#</strong><span>interaction, layout and edge geometry</span></div>
        <div><strong>80+ working examples</strong><span>most of them ports of React Flow's own</span></div>
        <div><strong>Every hosting model</strong><span>WebAssembly, Server, Web App and Hybrid</span></div>
        <div><strong>Keyboard &amp; screen reader</strong><span>every node and edge in the tab order</span></div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Feature grid ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Capabilities</p>
        <h2>Everything a node editor needs - as Blazor components</h2>
        <p class="bb-section-sub">
            Blow follows React Flow's concepts and, wherever C# allows, its names. The graph is plain C#: the same
            lists can be built, validated, laid out, copied and saved with no UI at all, which is what makes a flow
            testable and usable on a server.
        </p>
        <div class="bb-grid">
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Nodes and handles</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Built-in <code>input</code>, <code>default</code>, <code>output</code> and <code>group</code>
                    nodes, and a <code>NodeTemplate</code> for anything else - with <code>BlowHandle</code>s wherever
                    you put them, measured in the browser so edges always meet them.
                </p>
                <a href="custom-nodes" class="btn btn-outline">Custom nodes →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Six edge paths</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Bezier, simple bezier, straight, step, smooth step and routed paths that go round the nodes in their
                    way, each tunable per edge, with labels, arrowheads in any colour and size, animation, and
                    self-connections that loop clear of their node.
                </p>
                <a href="edge-types" class="btn btn-outline">Edge types →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Connections with rules</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Drag or click from handle to handle, with proximity snapping, duplicate, self-connection and cycle
                    rules, typed ports with connection limits, <code>IsValidConnection</code> on the canvas or on each
                    handle, and reconnection of existing edges.
                </p>
                <a href="examples/validation" class="btn btn-outline">Validation →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> Sub-flows and resizing</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Children move with their parent and can be pinned inside it or grow it, selections group and
                    ungroup in place, and a group collapses into itself; <code>BlowNodeResizer</code>, node and edge
                    toolbars and a portal into the zoomed layer sit right next to them.
                </p>
                <a href="advanced" class="btn btn-outline">Groups &amp; resizing →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Layout without JavaScript</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    <code>BlowLayout</code>'s layered (with crossing reduction), tree, radial, force-directed, grid and
                    circle layouts, the align and distribute commands of a drawing tool, and alignment guides while you
                    drag - all in C#.
                </p>
                <a href="examples/layouting" class="btn btn-outline">Layouting →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Undo, clipboard and saving</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    <code>HistoryEnabled</code> keeps the undo stack and binds Ctrl/Cmd+Z; copy and paste rewire edges
                    and keep groups intact; <code>ToObject()</code> and <code>RestoreAsync</code> save and reload a flow,
                    <code>ToJson</code> writes it as a versioned document and <code>ToSvg</code> as a picture.
                </p>
                <a href="examples/built-in-undo-redo" class="btn btn-outline">Built-in undo →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Validation and run states</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    <code>BlowGraphValidator</code> ties every problem to a node, edge or port, the canvas's
                    <code>Validation</code> marks them on the graph and <code>RunStates</code> shows a workflow running
                    step by step; <code>BlowGraphAlgorithms</code> adds topological order, cycles and shortest paths.
                </p>
                <a href="examples/workflow-runner" class="btn btn-outline">Workflow runner →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> Keyboard and screen readers</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Every node and edge is in the tab order: the arrow keys move, Enter selects, Delete removes and
                    <kbd>C</kbd> starts a connection, so a graph can be built without a pointer. Edits are announced,
                    and every string can be translated.
                </p>
                <a href="examples/keyboard-a11y" class="btn btn-outline">Keyboard access →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Themes and colour modes</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Every colour is a <code>--bit-blow-*</code> custom property with a dark twin; <code>ColorMode</code>
                    switches between light, dark and the system's choice, and Windows high contrast and reduced motion
                    are respected.
                </p>
                <a href="extras" class="btn btn-outline">Theming →</a>
            </section>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Code walkthrough ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">In practice</p>
        <h2>See it in code</h2>
        <div class="bb-walkthrough">
            <div class="bb-walkthrough-row">
                <div class="bb-walkthrough-copy">
                    <span class="bb-step">01</span>
                    <h3>Install and link the stylesheet</h3>
                    <p>
                        One package and one stylesheet link - there are no services to register, and the script module
                        is imported by the component the first time it needs it.
                    </p>
                    <a href="getting-started">Getting started →</a>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code">dotnet add package Bit.Blow

<span class="c">&lt;!-- App.razor or index.html --&gt;</span>
&lt;<span class="k">link</span> rel=<span class="s">"stylesheet"</span> href=<span class="s">"_content/Bit.Blow/bit-blow.css"</span> /&gt;

<span class="c">@@* _Imports.razor *@@</span>
<span class="k">@@using</span> Bit.Blow</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="bb-walkthrough-row">
                <div class="bb-walkthrough-copy">
                    <span class="bb-step">02</span>
                    <h3>Render your own nodes</h3>
                    <p>
                        A <code>NodeTemplate</code> switches on <code>node.Type</code>, with <code>BlowDefaultNode</code>
                        for the rest. Handles go wherever the markup puts them; give each its own <code>Id</code> to tell
                        an edge which one it starts from.
                    </p>
                    <a href="custom-nodes">Custom nodes →</a>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">BlowCanvas</span> Nodes=<span class="s">"_nodes"</span> Edges=<span class="s">"_edges"</span>&gt;
    &lt;<span class="k">NodeTemplate</span> Context=<span class="s">"node"</span>&gt;
        @@if (node.Type == <span class="s">"color"</span>)
        {
            &lt;<span class="k">BlowHandle</span> Type=<span class="s">"BlowHandleType.Target"</span> Position=<span class="s">"BlowPosition.Left"</span> /&gt;
            &lt;<span class="k">div</span>&gt;@@node.Label&lt;/<span class="k">div</span>&gt;
            &lt;<span class="k">BlowHandle</span> Type=<span class="s">"BlowHandleType.Source"</span> Id=<span class="s">"a"</span> Position=<span class="s">"BlowPosition.Right"</span> /&gt;
        }
        <span class="k">else</span>
        {
            &lt;<span class="k">BlowDefaultNode</span> Node=<span class="s">"node"</span> /&gt;
        }
    &lt;/<span class="k">NodeTemplate</span>&gt;
&lt;/<span class="k">BlowCanvas</span>&gt;</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="bb-walkthrough-row">
                <div class="bb-walkthrough-copy">
                    <span class="bb-step">03</span>
                    <h3>React to edits, drive it from code</h3>
                    <p>
                        The canvas edits your lists in place and raises <code>NodesChanged</code> once an edit is
                        complete - not on every pointer move. A <code>@@ref</code> is the imperative API, the counterpart
                        of <code>useReactFlow()</code>.
                    </p>
                    <a href="examples/use-reactflow">Use the flow API →</a>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">BlowCanvas</span> @@ref=<span class="s">"_flow"</span> Nodes=<span class="s">"_nodes"</span> Edges=<span class="s">"_edges"</span>
            NodesChanged=<span class="s">"Save"</span> OnConnect=<span class="s">"OnConnect"</span> /&gt;

_flow.FitView();
<span class="k">await</span> _flow.SetCenterAsync(x: 400, y: 200, zoom: 1.5, durationMs: 300);
BlowSnapshot snapshot = _flow.ToObject();   <span class="c">// nodes, edges and viewport</span></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Comparison ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Comparison</p>
        <h2>Coming from React Flow?</h2>
        <p class="bb-section-sub">
            The same concepts under the names a Blazor component gives them. The main difference is state: there are
            no change objects to apply - the canvas edits your lists in place and tells you afterwards, and a
            connection adds its own edge.
        </p>
        <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="React Flow compared with Bit.Blow">
            <table class="bb-table bb-compare">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Concern</th><th>React Flow</th><th>Bit.Blow</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>The component</td><td><code>&lt;ReactFlow&gt;</code></td><td><code>&lt;BlowCanvas&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>The imperative API</td><td><code>useReactFlow()</code></td><td>a <code>@@ref</code> to <code>BlowCanvas</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Applying edits</td><td><code>onNodesChange</code> + <code>applyNodeChanges</code></td><td>nothing to apply; <code>NodesChanged</code> after the edit</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Custom nodes</td><td><code>nodeTypes</code></td><td><code>NodeTemplate</code> switching on <code>node.Type</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Handles</td><td><code>&lt;Handle type="source" position={Position.Right} /&gt;</code></td><td><code>&lt;BlowHandle Type="BlowHandleType.Source" Position="BlowPosition.Right" /&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Auto-layout</td><td>dagre or elkjs, outside the library</td><td><code>BlowLayout</code>, built in</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Undo and redo</td><td>yours to write</td><td><code>HistoryEnabled</code>, or <code>BlowHistory</code> driven by you</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

