    @* ============================== FAQ ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Questions</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <div class="bb-faq">
            <details>
                <summary>Is this React Flow running through JavaScript interop?</summary>
                <p>
                    No. Dragging, selecting, connecting, resizing, keyboard handling, layout and edge geometry are written
                    in C# and run on Blazor's own event system. A small script module does only what the browser alone
                    can: measure DOM rectangles, notice when they change, find the handle under a point, hold pointer
                    capture and keep the page from scrolling under a gesture.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Which render modes does it work in?</summary>
                <p>
                    All of them, on .NET 8, 9 and 10. WebAssembly is the recommended model for editing - every pointer
                    move is handled in the browser. Server works with the same features, a round trip per move; Auto and
                    Hybrid work too. A statically rendered page shows the canvas but never becomes interactive.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Why does my canvas not show up?</summary>
                <p>
                    The canvas fills its container, so the container needs a height - or give the canvas one with
                    <code>Style</code>. And the stylesheet is not injected for you: without the
                    <code>_content/Bit.Blow/bit-blow.css</code> link the canvas renders unstyled.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>How large a graph can it handle?</summary>
                <p>
                    Every node's position is resolved once per render and edge paths are cached, so a pan rebuilds no
                    path strings. With <code>OnlyRenderVisibleElements</code> only what intersects the view is rendered -
                    the stress example builds grids of up to 900 nodes.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does it have a dark mode?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes: <code>ColorMode</code> is <code>Light</code>, <code>Dark</code> or <code>System</code>, and every
                    colour - the canvas, the control bar, the minimap, toolbars and labels - is a <code>--bit-blow-*</code>
                    custom property, so a theme is a handful of overrides. This site's flows follow its theme toggle.
                </p>
            </details>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Final CTA ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-final-cta">
        <h2>Draw the first edge</h2>
        <p class="bb-section-sub">One package, one stylesheet, a container with a height - and your app has a node editor.</p>
        <pre class="bb-code bb-hero-install">dotnet add package Bit.Blow</pre>
        <div class="bb-hero-actions">
